{
	title: 'Opt-in local terminal repaint tracing',
	spec: 'Terminal repaint diagnostics are disabled by default and enabled explicitly by an optional plugin in the terminal client home plugins/ directory, including for hal -r. A browser or remote host setting never enables tracing on another machine. A small client-only module starts and stops tracing through existing mutable module-object hooks without changing renderer output, geometry, colors, timing policy, or emergency keys. Its owner-only local JSONL file records timestamps, process ID, initiating client event/heartbeat/keyboard/resize/redraw sources (including sources coalesced by the paint throttle), frame row counts, first changed row, writable boundary, force/fullscreen flags, cursor coordinates, and whether paint parts contain a scrollback clear or cursor-home. It distinguishes forced rebuilds, physical shrink, changes to immutable rows, ordinary diffs, and unchanged frames. Never record transcript text, tool arguments/results, keys typed, session names/IDs, URLs, credentials, hostnames, paths, or raw terminal bytes. Importing the module has no side effects; stopping restores owned hooks without overwriting subsequent replacements. Trace files reject symlinks and non-regular files, use mode 0600, and tracing stops at a bounded record count or on write failure without disrupting the terminal. Configuration instructions are generic examples in this task artifact; actual local configuration and trace output remain private and untracked. Behavioral tests verify default inertness, causal attribution, clear-vs-diff distinction, content exclusion, bounded logging, and hook cleanup. The plugin registers activation/deactivation callbacks, performs no I/O or hook installation during import or staged registration, starts tracing only in the local -r process on activation, and stops it on reload, removal or expiry. A failed staged registration must not stop the active capture. Configuration examples use URL-safe file paths and create the private state directory when needed; local.ts is not required.',
